1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,140
Leave €10,000 alone for 30 years,

2
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:06,447
and it turns into about €76,000.

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:09,682
At 7% a year, you don't add a single euro.

4
00:00:09,722 --> 00:00:11,350
The money does the work.

5
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:13,739
That's compound interest.

6
00:00:13,779 --> 00:00:16,748
Slow, then boring, then suddenly unbelievable.

7
00:00:16,788 --> 00:00:19,171
Most people quit during the boring part.

8
00:00:19,733 --> 00:00:21,193
Seven ideas.

9
00:00:21,233 --> 00:00:22,619
No formulas.

10
00:00:22,659 --> 00:00:24,621
So simple, it feels illegal.

11
00:00:25,633 --> 00:00:28,178
One. Interest on interest.

12
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:32,350
Say you have €100, and it grows 10% a year.

13
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:35,517
After one year, you have €110.

14
00:00:36,267 --> 00:00:39,542
Next year, you don't earn 10% on €100.

15
00:00:39,582 --> 00:00:41,993
You earn it on €110.

16
00:00:42,033 --> 00:00:44,745
Your interest starts earning its own interest.

17
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:46,797
It's a snowball.

18
00:00:46,837 --> 00:00:48,601
Small at the top of the hill.

19
00:00:48,641 --> 00:00:50,123
Huge at the bottom.

20
00:00:50,167 --> 00:00:52,055
Same snow, same slope.

21
00:00:52,095 --> 00:00:54,341
The only thing that changed is time.

22
00:00:55,367 --> 00:00:58,044
Two. The rule of 72.

23
00:00:58,733 --> 00:01:01,156
Want to know how fast your money doubles?

24
00:01:01,196 --> 00:01:03,983
Divide 72 by the yearly return.

25
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:08,826
At 7% a year, 72 divided by 7 is about 10.

26
00:01:08,866 --> 00:01:11,542
Your money doubles roughly every ten years.

27
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:16,970
So €10,000 becomes about €20,000, then €39,000,

28
00:01:17,010 --> 00:01:20,797
then €76,000, the number from the start.

29
00:01:20,867 --> 00:01:23,707
Each doubling is bigger than all the ones before it.

30
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:27,132
Three. Time beats money.

31
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:29,323
Meet Anna and Ben.

32
00:01:29,363 --> 00:01:33,254
Both invest €200 a month, at 7% a year.

33
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:35,372
Anna starts at 25.

34
00:01:35,412 --> 00:01:37,409
Ben starts at 35.

35
00:01:37,449 --> 00:01:39,392
Both stop at 65.

36
00:01:40,067 --> 00:01:42,788
Ben puts in €72,000.

37
00:01:42,828 --> 00:01:46,324
He ends with about €244,000.

38
00:01:46,567 --> 00:01:49,513
Anna puts in only €24,000 more.

39
00:01:49,553 --> 00:01:53,242
She ends with about €525,000.

40
00:01:53,467 --> 00:01:56,222
Even if Ben doubles it to €400 a month,

41
00:01:56,262 --> 00:01:59,959
he ends with about €488,000.

42
00:01:59,999 --> 00:02:01,603
Still less than Anna.

43
00:02:02,367 --> 00:02:05,244
Anna ends with more than twice as much as Ben.

44
00:02:05,284 --> 00:02:07,521
Starting early beats starting big.

45
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:10,785
Four. The boring years.

46
00:02:11,467 --> 00:02:13,559
Here's why most people give up.

47
00:02:13,599 --> 00:02:15,932
After ten years of €200 a month,

48
00:02:15,972 --> 00:02:18,971
Anna has about €35,000.

49
00:02:19,033 --> 00:02:22,210
Only about €11,000 of that is growth.

50
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:24,386
It feels like nothing is happening.

51
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,445
But around year 11, something flips.

52
00:02:27,485 --> 00:02:28,675
One year of growth beats

53
00:02:28,715 --> 00:02:30,637
everything she puts in that year.

54
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:33,126
After that, the curve bends upward.

55
00:02:33,166 --> 00:02:35,384
The boring part was the foundation.

56
00:02:36,467 --> 00:02:39,082
Five. It works against you too.

57
00:02:39,767 --> 00:02:42,527
Compound interest doesn't care which side you're on.

58
00:02:42,567 --> 00:02:46,177
Store credit and credit cards can charge 20% a year.

59
00:02:46,867 --> 00:02:50,215
At 20%, a debt doubles in less than four years.

60
00:02:50,255 --> 00:02:53,165
That's the rule of 72, working for the bank.

61
00:02:53,367 --> 00:02:56,346
So before you invest, pay off expensive debt.

62
00:02:56,386 --> 00:02:58,637
It's the best return you'll ever get.

63
00:02:59,733 --> 00:03:02,181
Six. Fees compound too.

64
00:03:02,833 --> 00:03:06,078
A fee of 1.8% a year sounds tiny.

65
00:03:06,118 --> 00:03:07,220
It isn't.

66
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:10,340
€200 a month for 30 years:

67
00:03:10,380 --> 00:03:15,591
with fees of 0.2%, about €235,000.

68
00:03:15,631 --> 00:03:20,099
With 1.8%, about €173,000.

69
00:03:20,667 --> 00:03:21,994
Same money in.

70
00:03:22,034 --> 00:03:24,676
About €62,000 less out.

71
00:03:24,716 --> 00:03:27,778
The fee compounds against you, every single year.

72
00:03:28,867 --> 00:03:32,319
Seven. Inflation is compounding in reverse.

73
00:03:33,067 --> 00:03:35,302
Prices rise a little every year.

74
00:03:35,342 --> 00:03:38,267
The European Central Bank aims for 2%.

75
00:03:38,467 --> 00:03:39,972
At 2% inflation,

76
00:03:40,012 --> 00:03:43,153
cash loses about 45% of its buying power

77
00:03:43,193 --> 00:03:44,427
in 30 years.

78
00:03:45,067 --> 00:03:47,619
The same goes for every number in this video.

79
00:03:47,659 --> 00:03:48,732
In today's money,

80
00:03:48,772 --> 00:03:54,056
that €76,000 from the start is worth about €42,000.

81
00:03:54,096 --> 00:03:56,205
Still four times what went in.

82
00:03:56,767 --> 00:03:59,317
That's why long-term money can't just sit still.

83
00:03:59,357 --> 00:04:01,757
Over the last 125 years,

84
00:04:01,797 --> 00:04:04,112
global stocks grew about 5%

85
00:04:04,152 --> 00:04:05,677
a year faster than inflation,

86
00:04:05,717 --> 00:04:08,231
on average. Not every year.

87
00:04:08,271 --> 00:04:09,877
Some years were terrible.

88
00:04:09,917 --> 00:04:12,624
But over decades, time did the heavy lifting.

89
00:04:13,700 --> 00:04:15,129
Final message.

90
00:04:15,767 --> 00:04:18,027
Compound interest isn't magic.

91
00:04:18,067 --> 00:04:20,387
It's just patience, written in maths.

92
00:04:21,067 --> 00:04:22,324
Start early.

93
00:04:22,364 --> 00:04:24,192
Start small if you have to.

94
00:04:24,232 --> 00:04:25,589
Keep fees low.

95
00:04:25,629 --> 00:04:27,391
Kill expensive debt.

96
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,756
And then, the hardest part:

97
00:04:29,796 --> 00:04:31,880
do nothing, for a very long time.

98
00:04:32,667 --> 00:04:35,522
This is education, not financial advice.

99
00:04:35,562 --> 00:04:38,262
The numbers here are examples, not promises.

100
00:04:38,302 --> 00:04:40,070
If this helped, subscribe.

101
00:04:40,110 --> 00:04:43,945
Next up: how to save money so easily it feels illegal.
